"# Appendix C — The default search path (there is none)

The other dialect resolves a module name by walking an ordered list of directories — cache, user dir, system dir, project dir, current dir, then a remote registry. **Motoi searches for nothing.** There is no `import`, so there is no module name to resolve; the standard library is compiled in and loaded at boot. Every path verb the other dialect offers — `module-path`, `module-path-add!`, `module-path-prepend!`, `module-path-set!`, `module-find`, `module-manifest` — is `error: unbound symbol` in Motoi. This appendix is a dialect boundary: it records that the whole search-path machinery has no counterpart here.

The one runnable Motoi truth: a library verb is present with no search and no path, because it was never on a disk to be found.

```scheme
(list (string-append \"no\" \"-\" \"search\") (iota 3))
;; ⇒ (\"no-search\" (0 1 2))
```

## There is no default order

The other dialect tries `~/.motoi/cache/`, then `~/.motoi/modules/`, then a system dir, then `./modules/`, then `./`, then a registry — first match wins. **Motoi has no such order** because it looks for no file when you call a verb. The verb is already bound. There is nothing to try and nothing to win.

## There is no path to adjust

`(module-path)`, `(module-path-add! \"...\")`, `(module-path-prepend! ...)`, and `(module-path-set! ...)` all yield `error: unbound symbol` in Motoi. There is no list to read and no list to mutate. If you want to load *your own* source, you point the CLI at a file — `./bin/motoi run yourfile.scm` — and its top-level `define`s become available. That is file loading, not a module search path.

## There is no `$MOTOI_MODULE_PATH`

The other dialect prepends entries from a `MOTOI_MODULE_PATH` environment variable. Motoi reads no such variable for module resolution, because it resolves no modules. Setting it does nothing.

## There is no `module-find` debugger

`(module-find 'name)` reported where the other dialect looked and what it found. **Motoi binds no `module-find`** — it is `unbound`. The Motoi way to find out whether a name is available is to use it: an available verb runs; an unavailable one yields `error: unbound symbol: <name>`. That error *is* the \"not found\" report, and its message names exactly what was missing.

## There is no file-to-module mapping

The other dialect maps module `graphics/2d` to file `graphics/2d.scm` on a search-path root. Motoi has no module names, so there is no name-to-file convention. A file is just a file you hand to the interpreter; its definitions are not addressed by a module name.

## There are no manifests

A `MANIFEST.scm` let the other dialect remap module names to unusual file locations. Motoi consults no manifest for resolution because it resolves nothing. (Motoi books use a `MANIFEST.slat` as a table of contents for a book — a different, documentation-only thing, unrelated to module search.)

## When to touch the path

Never — there is nothing to touch. Where the other dialect said \"reach for `module-path-add!` rarely,\" Motoi says you reach for it *never*, because it is unbound. To use your own code, load your file; to use library code, just call it.

## Try this

Run the one-liner above and confirm two libraries' verbs work with no path and no search. Then type `(module-path)` and `(module-find 'anything)` and confirm both are `error: unbound symbol` — the search-path layer simply does not exist, because Motoi searches for nothing.")
